2348: Cost stays one mutant per de-fatalised predicate, not per row (#448 = 4 builds, #449 = 1).
2349: **Where the reading is uncertain, build BOTH** - #451 proved both directions measurable in
2350: minutes, and a spare build is cheaper than a wrong reading.
2351: 
2352: > ### *** THE ASK-AGAIN, ADOPTED 2026-08-22, AND ITS CAP IS THE WHOLE SAFETY. ***
2353: >
2354: > **Once a detector's kill table is CLEAN, ask the panel once more for another mutant.**
2355: > Measured yield: 2 of the 5 rows #451 added, and 6 of the 15 in #452, came only from that
2356: > second ask - after the first table was already green. The mechanism is real: a clean table
2357: > redirects seats away from the shapes the author already thought of.
2358: >
2359: > **CAPPED AT ONE ASK-AGAIN PER DETECTOR PER ROUND.** This is not the #392 chain - #392 was
2360: > unbounded fix-of-fix passes with no stop condition; this is a single extra adversarial query
2361: > against a clean table with a measured stop: nothing found, done; something found, row added
2362: > under L1, done. **The cap IS the anti-#392 clause.** A second ask-again after an empty first
2363: > is exactly where the chain starts, and anything found past the cap is FILED, never fixed.
2364: >
2365: > > ### *** THE CAP COUNTS **ASKS**, NEVER **FINDINGS**. Adjudicated 2026-08-28, because the
2366: > > ### singular in the sentence above ("row added", "something found") invited the opposite
2367: > > ### reading and that reading NEARLY FILED A FLOOR BREACH. ***
2368: > >
2369: > > Everything ONE ask returns is disposed of INDIVIDUALLY under L1-L4 and the floor - and **a
2370: > > floor breach among the findings is L2, repaired in-round however late it is found.** A
2371: > > second ASK is where the #392 chain starts; the number of rows one ask yields is not.
2372: > >
2373: > > **The founding measurement already said so and the wording contradicted it:** this block's
2374: > > own text records *"6 of the 15 in #452 came only from that second ask."* A reading under
2375: > > which #452 should have taken one row and filed five is refuted by the paragraph that mints
2376: > > the rule.
2377: > >
2378: > > **The live cost of the misreading, from `#488`:** three escapes were found by one ask; the
2379: > > author took one and filed two, reasoning "the cap says I may take only one". `E1` was
2380: > > `if (X)` -> `if (X && Y)` on the DOMINATING predicate - the canonical clause-(d) form, i.e.
2381: > > a floor breach, which the floor says must be repaired in-round. **So one rule's cap was
2382: > > about to silently override another rule's MUST.** A reading that produces that collision is
2383: > > wrong on its face.
2384: > >
2385: > > The anti-runaway property is untouched: the bounded resource was always the adversarial
2386: > > QUERY, and what it returns is bounded by being the findings of ONE query against ONE clean
2387: > > table.
