2480: 
2481: > ### THE FOUR REASONS THAT LICENSE AN IN-ROUND FIX OF DETECTOR MATERIAL
2482: >
2483: > Everything else is FILED. Named so no round has to re-argue it:
2484: >
2485: > * **L1 - pre-ship construction.** The row is part of the deliverable still being built. A row
2486: >   measured vacuous before commit is unfinished, not a reopening. **The stopping rule governs
2487: >   findings against SHIPPED material only.**
2488: > * **L2 - floor breach.** The shipped detector fails vacuity-floor clause (a), (b) or (c) -
2489: >   replace in-round however late it is found.
2490: > * **L3 - undischarged obligation.** The row is the sole carrier of something the round OWES
2491: >   (a de-escalation accept row; the "shipped a detector" claim itself). A vacuous sole carrier
2492: >   makes the round's record false.
2493: > * **L4 - wrong record.** Prose or comments claiming coverage a measurement refutes.
2494: 
2495: This resolves an apparent inconsistency: #447's escapes were FILED while #448's and #449's were
2496: fixed in-round. #447's were measured against a **shipped** detector sitting above the floor -
2497: coverage debt. #448's and #449's were caught **inside their own construction** (L1), and #448's
2498: `return``break` variant additionally re-reached the removed `exit(1)`, which is L2.
2499: 
2500: **A disjointness assertion over the latch classes was REJECTED, on three measured grounds** - the
2501: class bit-domains overlap pervasively today, so the general form is unsatisfiable; separation
2502: rests on the array INDICES rather than on the constants, so the assert would guard the wrong
2503: invariant; and for the one disjoint pair the values are hardware register layouts that cannot
2504: legitimately change, so it could never fire. **The real hazard is a duplicate class ID**, and its
2505: zero-cost structural fix is converting the `SH4_VAL_*` defines to a C99 enum ending in
FILE: OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md
22352: probe on all four timers AND both gate-2 differentials (the Opus seat, measured; EXTERNAL by reading).  M_nper brings
22353: #442's dead clock back at rung 3.  The cause is structural: the probe writes 0x88 and 0x00, gate 2 arms 0x80 and 0x88,
22354: and `diff_footbridge.c` sets PERIODIC but never calls the callbacks, although DEVINIT resets every timer to periodic.
22355: Cheapest fix (the Opus seat): a periodic and an FCLK_16 case in gate 2's R1 table -- three rows each, IDENT moves, and
22356: a re-table.
22357: 
22358: ### `fbharnessedits` (LOW, harness) - edits to the instruments' own logic that keep every `#548` pin green
22359: 
22360: `#548` pins what is cheap and exact (the timer/load/ctrl line, the UNCAPPED line, one D and one X backlog in range, the
22361: whole R6/R6b lines) and declares the rest "reviewed, not rowed".  The rest, each read or measured by the seat named:
22362: the probe's TBASE rebound to timer 0 while printing the requested timer (codex); `free_run(0.4)` inside the probe
22363: (codex); `backlog(D, ranX)` for X (codex; the kimi seat asked that this class carry a tag); `add1("sl")` ->
22364: `mov("sl", 1)` publishing a constant 1 (codex); the X/D print labels (codex, measured in-memory); R6's owed count bumped
22365: after sampling, or zeroed after sampling (codex); R6b's two loops merged (codex); a row renamed while its pin reads only
22366: the detail (codex -- closed by `#548`'s whole-line pins); Opus's three-token R6 edit (driving timer 0 for 3810 ticks and
22367: reading the end from `[0]`).  Also recorded here because it is the same kind of limit: `Session.wait()` restarts its
22368: timeout on any output, so continuous output without a prompt can hold the probe indefinitely (codex x2, fake-clock
22369: fixtures) and nothing outside the probe times it in gate 14.
22370: 
22371: ### `fbdrainxdisable` (LOW, harness) - nothing checks, per run, that the drain probe's arm X was disabled
22372: 
22373: Arm X's disable and re-enable words (0x54/0x58, 0x70/0x74) free-run and are never compared with the disassembly (arm E
22374: steps arm D's program), and on a correct build X reads 3, exactly like D.  #548's M_en (X = 3051 against D = 3) is the
22375: evidence the disable ran in that measurement; nothing repeats it per run, and X is the battery's only gated witness of
22376: a bound gated on TIMER_ENABLE.  Fix: a control row reading TIMER_x_CONTROL back after the disable, or step those words.
22377: 
22378: ### `fbdrainonepass` (LOW, harness) - a single-process four-timer drain mode would halve gate 14's new cost

